  #line-at(49.56pt, 354.41pt, "Abstract ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 370.01pt, "The optical response of a suspension microdroplet is governed not only by the properties of the dispersed phase, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 384.53pt, "but also by the finite size and optical structure of the droplet itself. As a result, the interpretation of scattered-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 399.05pt, "light patterns from such systems constitutes a non-trivial inverse problem. In this work, we examine whether ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 413.59pt, "laser speckle images recorded from single levitating microdroplets of suspension can be used for data-driven ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 428.23pt, "recognition of selected droplet and suspension parameters. Experiments were performed on slowly evaporating ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 442.75pt, "microdroplets of monodisperse TiO")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(208.97pt, 444.31pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(212.45pt, 442.75pt, " nanoparticle suspensions in diethylene glycol confined in a linear electro-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 457.27pt, "dynamic quadrupole trap. Speckle images were analyzed with a convolutional neural network trained to classify ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 471.79pt, "droplet diameter, nanoparticle concentration, and nanoparticle diameter, first in separate tasks and then in com-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 486.43pt, "bined two-parameter and three-parameter classifications. Under the present experimental conditions, droplet ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 500.95pt, "diameter was identified with good reliability, with an estimated accuracy better than approximately 6% for the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 515.47pt, "tested dataset. Nanoparticle concentration was more difficult to resolve, but useful discrimination was obtained ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 529.99pt, "when concentration classes were sufficiently separated. Nanoparticle diameter was also classified unambiguous-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 544.51pt, "ly for the selected cases. In addition, simultaneous classification of up to three parameters across 27 classes was ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 559.15pt, "achieved. These results suggest that CNN-based analysis of speckle images may provide a viable route toward ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 573.7pt, "multi-parameter optical diagnostics of free suspension microdroplets and, potentially, more complex aerosol-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 588.22pt, "like systems. ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 14.04pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#365f91"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 629.98pt, "Introduction ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 645.7pt, "The optical response of a suspension microdroplet is determined jointly by the properties of the dispersed phase ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 660.22pt, "and by the fact that the droplet itself is a finite dielectric object. In contrast to a bulk suspension, a microdroplet ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 674.74pt, "modifies the incident field, and may therefore act as a (weak) optical resonator supporting morphology-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 689.26pt, "dependent resonances [1], (compare [2]). As a consequence, the total scattered field need not reflect only the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 703.9pt, "properties of the suspended particles, but may be strongly shaped by the droplet-scale field distribution. This ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 718.42pt, "makes the retrieval of suspension and droplet parameters from recorded optical patterns a non-trivial inverse ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 732.94pt, "problem.")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 757.56pt, "Single-particle levitation methods, and electrodynamic balances in particular, provide an attractive platform for ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 772.08pt, "studying such systems. They enable non-contact trapping and long observation of individual droplets, while ")
]

// arXiv source page 2
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, (image("graphics/page-02.png", width: 401.5pt), 143.5pt, 497pt))[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 59.52pt, "allowing simultaneous optical access for imaging, light-scattering measurements and spectroscopic probing [3–")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 74.16pt, "6] In aerosol and droplet physics, these methods have become important tools for investigating evaporation, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 88.7pt, "hygroscopicity, morphology, phase transitions and other physicochemical properties of isolated particles. In the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 103.22pt, "present context, electrodynamic levitation offers the additional advantage that slowly evolving microdroplets of ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 117.74pt, "suspension can be observed under well-controlled conditions, making them suitable model systems for testing ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 132.26pt, "optical recognition strategies. ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 156.86pt, "Laser speckle is particularly sensitive to slight changes in the structure of the scattering object. Because speckle ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 171.38pt, "arises from the interference of many partial wave contributions, its spatial structure can encode subtle infor-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 185.9pt, "mation about the geometry of the droplet, the effective optical properties of the medium, and the presence, dis-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 200.54pt, "tribution and properties of suspended nanoparticles [7], (compare e.g. [8]). At the same time, this very sensitivi-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 215.06pt, "ty makes its interpretation difficult: the relevant information is distributed over a complex stochastic texture ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 229.58pt, "rather than contained in a small number of easily identifiable observables (see e.g. [9]).")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 254.21pt, "This type of problem is well suited to convolutional neural networks (CNNs), which can learn discriminative ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 268.73pt, "image features directly from experimental data and combine them hierarchically into task-relevant representa-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 283.25pt, "tions [10–12] In image-analysis tasks where the informative content is texture-like and spatially distributed, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 297.77pt, "CNNs are often more effective than manually designed descriptors because they do not require one to identify in ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 312.41pt, "advance which image features are physically most relevant (compare [13]). Their use is therefore particularly ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 326.93pt, "appealing for speckle-based classification, where visually obvious differences between classes may be weak or ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 341.45pt, "absent.")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 366.05pt, "In our previous publications we showed that it is possible to identify suspensions in bulk by classification of ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 380.57pt, "laser speckle images they generate, with the help of a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN). First these were ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 395.09pt, "monodisperse nanoparticle suspensions in water (the suspended particle type and concentration) [14], then real-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 409.63pt, "world suspensions exemplified by cow milk (fat contents) [15]. We also showed that it is possible to formulate a ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 424.15pt, "classification task to follow the diameter evolution of droplets of pure liquids, and, to some extent, liquid mix-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 438.79pt, "tures and suspensions [16].  Consequently, we wanted to find out if it is possible to recognize other parameters ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 453.31pt, "of microdroplets of suspension, associated with their composition (suspended phase concentration, particle size, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 467.83pt, "etc.) with the same method. This was not so obvious, since the light scattering on particles in a microdroplet is ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 482.35pt, "controlled by light field distribution imposed by the microdroplet – acting as an optical resonator. The total scat-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 496.87pt, "tered ")
  #line-at(85.308pt, 496.87pt, "field ")
  #line-at(119.123pt, 496.87pt, "can ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 511.51pt, "easily be dominat-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 526.03pt, "ed by scattering on ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 540.55pt, "“effective” droplet ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 555.07pt, "as a whole and the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 569.71pt, "detailed ")
  #line-at(105.091pt, 569.71pt, "effects ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 584.26pt, "introduced by sus-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 598.78pt, "pended phase can ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 613.3pt, "be lost [17]. In the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 627.82pt, "present work, we ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 642.46pt, "address this ques-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 656.98pt, "tion experimental-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 671.5pt, "ly ")
  #line-at(75.096pt, 671.5pt, "for ")
  #line-at(105.058pt, 671.5pt, "slowly ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 686.02pt, "evaporating ")
  #line-at(114.21pt, 686.02pt, "levi-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 700.66pt, "tated ")
  #line-at(105.102pt, 700.66pt, "micro-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 715.18pt, "droplets of mono-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 729.7pt, "disperse TiO")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(110.18pt, 731.26pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(113.66pt, 729.7pt, " na-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 744.24pt, "noparticle suspen-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 758.76pt, "sions in diethylene ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 773.4pt, "glycol. This sys-")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#4f81bd"))
  #line-at(143.42pt, 765pt, "Figure 1 Experimental setup for recording laser speckle images from single levitating ")
  #line-at(143.42pt, 778.32pt, "microdroplets of suspension. ")
