  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 59.52pt, "centration manifests in some finer speckle image features, which are not really distinguishable to a human ob-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 74.16pt, "server. First, we wanted to classify NPs mass concentration for a set droplet diameter. ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 98.66pt, "Since we could not exactly replicate the microdroplet diameter for different NPs concentrations (different exper-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 114.02pt, "imental runs), we first tried to select a single diameter for each NP concentration, which would fall into a ")
  #line-at(532.06pt, 114.02pt, "0.5 ")
  #set text(font: "Symbol", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 129.5pt, "m")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(55.92pt, 129.5pt, "m range, and tested the trained CNN also on very nearby diameters. This however didn’t yield sensible results ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 144.26pt, "– it indeed indicates that the droplet diameter is associated with too distinct features quickly changing even with ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 159.62pt, "a small variation of the droplet diameter. Thus we chose a wider range of ")
  #line-at(381.31pt, 159.62pt, "3 ")
  #set text(font: "Symbol", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(389.59pt, 159.62pt, "m")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(395.83pt, 159.62pt, "m, selected 3 fairly distant diame-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 174.26pt, "ters from the range and combined the corresponding speckle images into one class for CNN training. Then we ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 188.78pt, "tested the trained CNN on 3 different droplets from the selected range of diameters. An example result is shown ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 204.14pt, "in the left panel of Figure 7 for droplet diameters range of 65")
  #line-at(325.25pt, 204.14pt, "3 ")
  #set text(font: "Symbol", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(333.55pt, 204.14pt, "m")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(339.91pt, 204.14pt, "m. We found that for an unambiguous classifi-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 218.9pt, "cation, the concentration difference between classes by a factor of at least ~4 was required. This is only to be ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 233.42pt, "expected due to the low accuracy of initial suspension concentration estimation, leading to a significant spread ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 247.97pt, "of concentrations within a single class. Obviously, this could be further improved by training a CNN on suspen-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 262.49pt, "sions with better-defined concentrations. ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 11.04pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#4f81bd"))
  #line-at(63.72pt, 287.57pt, "Recognition of suspension type – nanoparticle size – in microdroplet ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 301.85pt, "In a complementary experiment we tried classification of microdroplets of suspensions of NPs of the same ma-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 316.49pt, "terial but of different diameters. In this case, it is more difficult to set parameters other than the NPs material ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 331.01pt, "and the range of the microdroplet diameters as fixed parameters. Keeping the number of NPs in a microdroplet ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 345.53pt, "constant versus significant changes of NP diameter leads to huge mass concentration differences. E.g., if we ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 360.05pt, "demand the same number of 100- and 500-nm-diameter NPs in the microdroplet of a given diameter, the mass ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 374.57pt, "concentration of the smaller NPs would have to be 125 times smaller, and their scattering cross-section ~25 ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 389.21pt, "times smaller leading to very dim speckles and poor brightness dynamics. This would inevitably introduce a ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 403.73pt, "high bias into the training. Thus, here we tested NPs diameter recognition for constant mass concentration, bear-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 418.27pt, "ing in mind that it causes mixing of the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 432.79pt, "influence of NP diameter and NPs num-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 447.43pt, "ber. To obtain a better generalization, the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 461.95pt, "procedure of composing classes from ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 476.47pt, "droplet radii ranges was somewhat ex-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 490.99pt, "tended: image series corresponding to 5 ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 506.35pt, "fairly distant diameters from the ")
  #line-at(210.17pt, 506.35pt, "4 ")
  #set text(font: "Symbol", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(220.37pt, 506.35pt, "m")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(226.73pt, 506.35pt, "m ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 521.11pt, "range were combined, and 4 different ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 535.63pt, "droplets from the same diameter range ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 550.15pt, "were used for construction of the inde-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 564.67pt, "pendent set. In the right panel of Figure 7, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 579.22pt, "we present tests with microdroplets from ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 594.7pt, "the range of diameters of 100")
  #line-at(186.5pt, 594.7pt, "4 ")
